# LEGACY-RME-PDF — Legacy RME archive (permanent rules)

1. A **legacy RME** is an archive of a patient's OLD RME data, sourced from a
   historical PDF. It is **not** a template and **not** an RME produced by the
   live examination workflow.
2. It is operated by Super Admin from **Master Data → Master Data RME → Import
   Arsip RME Lama**.
3. A legacy RME **never** creates a clinic visit. It has no `clinic_visit_id`
   and must never be forced into `trx_medical_records`, which requires one.
4. A legacy RME **never** creates or affects: invoices, payments, consent,
   odontogram, `LabCaseCandidate`, `LabOrder`, SATUSEHAT candidates/submissions,
   visit status, visit KPI or revenue KPI.
5. The legacy date is **chosen manually** by the operator from what is visible
   on the document. Never from the upload time, `created_at`, the file date, PDF
   metadata or OCR. It lives in `selected_rme_date` / `rme_date`, separate from
   `uploaded_at` and `published_at`.
6. **The bound (corrected by FIX-ROLL2-1 — see rule 95):** a document is a date
   RANGE. `selected_rme_date` is the EARLIEST clinical date, `latest_rme_date`
   the latest. WHEN the patient has a native RME, EVERY date the document
   represents must be STRICTLY earlier than it (`latest < earliest native RME`;
   equal is refused). Also `latest < today` (today is refused), and
   `patient birth date <= earliest` when a birth date exists (equal to the
   birth date is accepted; `date_of_birth` is nullable, so a missing value skips
   that rule and is never invented).
7. **A patient with NO native RME is ELIGIBLE** (corrected by FIX-ROLL2-1). The
   native RME is a BOUND, not a PREREQUISITE: most migrated patients have no
   native RME at all. Never manufacture a visit or medical record to create a
   comparison date. The old `PATIENT_HAS_NO_NATIVE_RME` refusal is REMOVED; the
   fact is kept as evidence in the reference mode (`BEFORE_NATIVE_RME` /
   `NO_NATIVE_REFERENCE`).
8. `earliest_native_rme_date` comes from **`trx_clinic_visits.visit_date`** via
   the visit that owns the medical record — never `trx_medical_records`'
   `created_at`, `finalized_at` or `canonical_visit_id`, and never
   `mst_patients.registered_at`.
9. The cutoff is computed **only** in
   `App\Modules\LegacyRme\Services\PatientEarliestNativeRmeDateResolver`
   (backed by `ClinicVisitRepository::earliestVisitWithMedicalRecordForPatient`).
   No controller, form request, policy, view or test may re-derive it.
10. The cutoff excludes cancelled visits, visits with no medical record,
    soft-deleted visits/records and **every** legacy row (staged, published or
    VOID). A legacy record is never a native record.
11. The cutoff is deliberately **not branch-scoped** — a narrower scan could only
    move the bound later and admit an overlapping document. Do not "fix" this to
    match `PatientRmWorkspaceResolver`.
12. All date rules live in `LegacyRmeDateRuleService` with stable codes:
    `LEGACY_DATE_NOT_BEFORE_NATIVE_RME`, `LEGACY_DATE_IN_FUTURE`,
    `LEGACY_DATE_BEFORE_PATIENT_BIRTH`, `LEGACY_DATE_INVALID`,
    `LEGACY_DATE_RANGE_INVALID`. Branch on the code, never on the message. The
    cutoff is always recomputed server-side; a client-supplied snapshot is never
    trusted.
13. A **published** legacy record is immutable: no in-place edit of patient,
    date, file, hash or pages, and no hard delete (the published tables carry no
    soft delete either). The only correction is **VOID with a reason plus a fresh
    import**. `UNIQUE(source_import_id)` keeps publishing idempotent.
14. The **caller's** access is resolved server-side by `LegacyRmeWorkspaceScope`
    from `BranchContext` / the RME-enabled branch set, fail-closed on an
    unresolvable scope, and **never** from the request.
    But `origin_branch_id` on the ROW is not merely descriptive: repository
    listings filter on it and the policy scopes on it, so it decides which branch
    owns and can see that row. It is therefore validated on write against the
    **uploader's own scope**, not just the global RME-enabled set — otherwise a
    scoped operator could file a document into a branch they have no authority
    over and then 404 on their own row. Rows with no origin branch are
    governance-tier only, so a scoped operator's upload is anchored to their own
    branch rather than left branchless.
15. Permissions are `view_legacy_rme_imports`, `create_legacy_rme_imports`,
    `review_legacy_rme_imports`, `publish_legacy_rme_imports`,
    `void_legacy_rme_imports`. They are granted to no operational role by
    default; Super Admin holds them through the existing `'*'` grant plus the
    single global `Gate::before`. Never create permission rows outside
    `PermissionSeeder`.
16. Full KTP/NIK is never rendered, exported or written into an audit payload.
    Audit goes to the shared `sys_audit_logs` trail through
    `LegacyRmeAuditService`, which filters every payload against
    `LegacyRmeAuditEvent::ALLOWED_METADATA_KEYS` and drops non-scalars — never a
    patient name, clinical content, base64 data, a raw PDF or an absolute path.
17. Legacy files are private. Never the public disk, never a public URL, never
    `storage:link`. Serving is only ever the authenticated, policy-checked
    streaming controller.
18. Migrations for this domain are **additive only** — `php artisan migrate`,
    never `migrate:fresh`, never `db:wipe`.
19. The capability is gated by the feature flag `rme.legacy_pdf_archive`
    (default **OFF**), resolved through `LegacyRmeFeatureGuard` /
    `FeatureFlagService` — never `config('feature_flags…')`, whose dots break
    traversal.
20. Source of truth order stays: current code and migrations → current tests →
    current routes and policies → repository rules → current docs → older docs.

